      "text": "To solve the problem, we need to determine the equation of the line passing through the points \\((2, 2, 1)\\) and \\((5, 1, -2)\\). Then, we need to find the \\(z\\)-coordinate of a point on this line where the \\(x\\)-coordinate is 4.\n\n### Step 1: Find the direction vector of the line\nThe direction vector \\(\\vec{d}\\) of the line can be found by subtracting the coordinates of the first point from the coordinates of the second point:\n\\[\n\\vec{d} = (5 - 2, 1 - 2, -2 - 1) = (3, -1, -3)\n\\]\n\n### Step 2: Write the parametric equations of the line\nThe parametric equations of a line passing through a point \\((x_0, y_0, z_0)\\) with direction vector \\((a, b, c)\\) are:\n\\[\nx = x_0 + at\n\\]\n\\[\ny = y_0 + bt\n\\]\n\\[\nz = z_0 + ct\n\\]\nUsing the point \\((2, 2, 1)\\) and the direction vector \\((3, -1, -3)\\), we get:\n\\[\nx = 2 + 3t\n\\]\n\\[\ny = 2 - t\n\\]\n\\[\nz = 1 - 3t\n\\]\n\n### Step 3: Find the value of the parameter \\(t\\) when \\(x = 4\\)\nWe are given that the \\(x\\)-coordinate is 4. Substitute \\(x = 4\\) into the parametric equation for \\(x\\):\n\\[\n4 = 2 + 3t\n\\]\nSolving for \\(t\\):\n\\[\n4 - 2 = 3t \\implies 2 = 3t \\implies t = \\frac{2}{3}\n\\]\n\n### Step 4: Find the \\(z\\)-coordinate when \\(t = \\frac{2}{3}\\)\nSubstitute \\(t = \\frac{2}{3}\\) into the parametric equation for \\(z\\):\n\\[\nz = 1 - 3\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)\n\\]\n\\[\nz = 1 - 2\n\\]\n\\[\nz = -1\n\\]\n\nThus, the \\(z\\)-coordinate of the point on the line where the \\(x\\)-coordinate is 4 is \\(\\boxed{-1}\\).",
