      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To find the \\( z \\)-coordinate of a point on the line passing through \\((2,2,1)\\) and \\((5,1,-2)\\) with an \\( x \\)-coordinate of 4, we first need to determine the parametric equations of the line.\n\nThe direction vector \\(\\mathbf{d}\\) of the line can be found by subtracting the coordinates of the two given points:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{d} = (5-2, 1-2, -2-1) = (3, -1, -3)\n\\]\nThe parametric equations of the line can be written as:\n\\[\nx = 2 + 3t, \\quad y = 2 - t, \\quad z = 1 - 3t\n\\]\nWe are given that the \\( x \\)-coordinate of the point is 4. So, we substitute \\( x = 4 \\) into the parametric equation for \\( x \\):\n\\[\n4 = 2 + 3t\n\\]\nSolving for \\( t \\):\n\\[\n4 - 2 = 3t \\implies 2 = 3t \\implies t = \\frac{2}{3}\n\\]\nNow, we substitute \\( t = \\frac{2}{3} \\) into the parametric equation for \\( z \\):\n\\[\nz = 1 - 3t = 1 - 3 \\left( \\frac{2}{3} \\right) = 1 - 2 = -1\n\\]\nTherefore, the \\( z \\)-coordinate of the point is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{-1}\n\\]",
